# EcoService — AI Rules for Cursor / Claude / Copilot

## MANDATORY RULES (never violate)
- NEVER generate code without interfaces or abstract base classes
- ALWAYS write tests (TDD) BEFORE or alongside implementation
- NEVER use external databases (MongoDB, PostgreSQL, etc.) — use In-Memory repositories only
- NEVER use external APIs or HTTP calls in business logic
- ALWAYS implement Repository pattern for data access
- ALWAYS use dependency injection (pass dependencies via constructor)
- NEVER use Singleton pattern except for NotificationService
- EVERY service must receive its repositories via constructor

## ARCHITECTURE
- 4-layer architecture: Models → Repositories → Services → (Tests)
- Models: Pure domain objects, no business logic side effects
- Repositories: In-memory Map-based storage, extend BaseRepository
- Services: Business logic only, no direct storage access
- Utils: Strategies (GoF), EventEmitter (Observer), ID generation

## PATTERNS (GoF required)
- Strategy: Points calculation (StandardPointsStrategy, BonusMultiplierStrategy, etc.)
- Observer: NotificationService extends EventEmitter
- Repository: BaseRepository extended by domain-specific repos

## SOLID PRINCIPLES
- SRP: Each class has one responsibility
- OCP: Extend via strategies, not modification
- LSP: All strategies implement BasePointsStrategy interface
- ISP: Repositories have focused interfaces
- DIP: Services depend on abstractions, not concrete classes

## TESTING RULES
- Coverage threshold: 70% minimum (currently ~97%)
- Test file pattern: tests/**/*.test.js
- Use jest.fn() for mocks, never real HTTP calls
- Test edge cases: empty inputs, null, negative numbers, boundary values
- Integration tests go in tests/integration/

## FORBIDDEN
- require('axios'), require('mongoose'), require('sequelize'), require('knex')
- Any fetch() or HTTP client in src/
- Global state except resetCounters() in tests
- console.log in production code
